WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.674
These four illustrative markets need two,
four, three and five correction rounds.

2
00:00:05.674 --> 00:00:07.892
Their first stopping points differ.

3
00:00:07.892 --> 00:00:11.442
The solver must preserve those individual
stopping decisions.

4
00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:15.180
In a host-driven loop, the CPU repeatedly

5
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.818
launches a device update and checks
whether work remains.

6
00:00:18.818 --> 00:00:22.457
Many short rounds can add launch and
synchronization overhead.

7
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:27.330
As a market converges, freeze its result.

8
00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:29.033
The remaining markets continue.

9
00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:31.587
Requiring every market to run five

10
00:00:31.587 --> 00:00:34.567
effective updates would change this
stopping behavior.

11
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:41.005
A resident kernel keeps the correction
loop and active masks on the device.

12
00:00:41.005 --> 00:00:43.512
One launch can cover several rounds while

13
00:00:43.512 --> 00:00:45.660
retaining each market's first stopping
point.

14
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:52.366
The numerical target, precision and
stopping rule remain fixed.

15
00:00:52.366 --> 00:00:54.598
The saving is in coordination.

16
00:00:54.598 --> 00:00:59.506
Register use and occupancy may change, so
compare validated complete runs.
